  "language": "english",
  "text": "We are prepared to make you a very generous offer. And we are prepared to reject that offer. Michael, you haven't even heard that. They will never accept their first offer. What is your second offer? It's true that sportsbooks often give you a terrible cash out. So I'm going to show you how to figure out what a fair amount for your cash out should be. And it's pretty easy. Step one is figuring out what is the cash out amount that they're offering you and what would be the total payout if you were to win. So you can see in this example, my cash out offer is $179, but the total payout would be $358 if I win. Step two, see what the live line odds are for your exact same bet. So again, in this example, you can see that the Mariners are now minus 140. Now if you're betting a spread, just go to the alternate lines and see what the odds are for your line there. Step three is to take the live line odds and convert them into an implied probability. Now if you don't know how to do that, you can just Google implied probability calculator, or you can use these formulas here depending on if they're positive odds or negative odds. We also have a video on this if you want to learn more. So in this example, the Mariners are minus 140. So that's an implied probability of 58.3% or 0.583. And the last step, multiply the implied probability times the total amount that you would get paid out if you're bet one. So in this example, we multiply 358 by 0.583 and you get $209. So as you can see, I'm getting cheated out of $30 or 14% of what a fair payout should be. So before you hit that cash out button, ask yourself, are you getting a fair price? And if not, is it worth giving that money that you should be getting back to the book? Sometimes there's a huge injury or a change in a game where it may be worth cashing out, but the answer is almost always no. Also remember hedging your bet by betting the other side may be a viable option. All right, if that helped, be sure to give us a follow as we'll be giving you tips all throughout football season. And as always, good luck.",
